	How can we be too surprised about this when we have been so long
ignoring the 10th Amendment?  My region has been treated recently with
Federal bureaucrats playing politics with Virginia Beach's plan to pipe
water to the city from a river to the west, a pipeline entirely within the
state.  Congressional Committees are threatening to appropriate the
decisions of a local zoning board in the proposed Disney theme park.  It is
becoming harder and harder for any entity to act on anything without bowing,
sometimes repeatedly, to the federales.

	A federal government which will accept no limits in the matters it
controls creates the sort of measures you speak of.  This is so because
politicians can present these sorts of things as evidence of how he has
helped citizens, effectively buying those votes in the next election. 
Furthermore, interest groups (both industry types and ideological types) can
make more effective use of their lobbying money by concentrating power at
the federal level.  It's cheaper to maintain one office in D. C. than to
maintain 50 in the state capitals, or thousands at the city or county level,
if they can have the same legislative effect.

	If a lobbyist in DC can plausibly promise to deliver a) money, b)
many volunteers for the campaign, c) endorsements; how can you as a common
citizen compare?  What do your desires matter in comparison to his-and his
clients?  How can you impress your case if he's thousands of miles away from
we regular folks and the lobbyist is in town visiting weekly or even daily?

	The news media plays another major part in the problem.  They
complain endlessly about how politicians are controlled by special
interests, but they continue to present news stories in the same way-a)
identify __________ as a problem, b) describe the problem and the victims of
the putative problem sympathetically, c) state or imply that the government
is not doing enough, as though that is the only plausible and practical
solution.  It never occurs to them that if politicians make all decisions,
they will make those decisions using the criteria of politics.  Note the
bickering in the media and the Administration about how opposing groups (who
are labeled special interests, as compared to those who want to nationalize
health care-are they not special interests too?) are lobbying to shape the
bill.  Surprise, surprise!  Is there any intelligence at the White House?
Did they really not expect that when politicians make decisions about Health
Care that they would do so politically?

	Secondly, the media abhors communities that exercise some freedom to 
handle putative promlems in different ways consistent with local conditions
and community values.  NPR reported on an Iowa city which implemented a
program promoting homosexuality as a valid lifestyle choice with almost no
controversy, while a similar program in NY City cost the Superintendent his
job.  It seems to me that allowing local bodies, bodies of government closer
to the people, to make such decisions is a far wiser course.  Returning
power to levels of government where real citizens can make a difference to
 legislators (while making the practice of rent seeking through lobbying 
less practical) is key to taking our government back, and preventing such
things.differently.  
