Jane Elza <jelza@grits.valdosta.peachnet.edu> wrote:
>a student at a university set up an unauthorized listserv on humor, which 
>administration wants to close down. are there any cases on this? past 
>experience?

This can be a tough nut to crack.  On my systems, mailing lists are only 
established for faculty, staff or *registered student organizations*.  For 
instance, the Engineering Student Council has a list, as does the Society 
for Women Engineers.  I don't have the resources to handle an every-Joe-
Schmo-runs-a-list load of mail, so our students don't set up their own lists.

Is this really a mailing list, or is the student just sending broadcast
mail to a bunch of friends?  In other words, is there some address which
*external* folks can use to reach the list?  If it's a personal list of
contacts, rather than a globally accessible list, I wouldn't touch it
with the proverbial 10-foot-pole; it isn't the university's business to
see who Joe Schmo is sending email to...

If you don't allow *any* unofficial/student-run mailing lists, your path 
is clear.  If you do, however, you may be skirting close to content regu-
lation, which (in my opinion) is usually a massive no-no.

--Wes

