>I find homosexuality absolutely abhorrent 

You find it "absolutely abhorrent".  Ok, but this sentiment does not 
by itself bear any relevance to the issue of personal freedom.
I mean, I assume that since you post to an academic freedom newsgroup
that it is a subject in which you are interested.  There are probably
certain things that you believe to make worthwhile spending your time
to read this group.  Is there some argument that you have thought of
which relates to the basic assumption of most proponents of academic 
freedom that ideas should not be censored, or that students or teachers
should not be punished for expressing or studying ideas?  Is there 
anything that you have to add with which you might attempt to convince
someone that the above statement is more than just an emotional attitude,
and somehow a rational conclusion or a demonstratable fact?  

What qualifies this attitude to be a proper consideration in the making 
of public policy?  I mean, I might decide that "Rush Limbaugh is disgusting"
and "should be banned from the airwaves", but the only connection I have
provided between these two propositions is a *very tenuous* 'and'.  
Emotional attitudes are a part of our nature, but it is very difficult to
show how they, alone, have any logical connection to what is factually 
true, or provide us with axioms that are consistently applicable to the 
large-scale maintenance of human happiness.   

>and a perversion that is to be condemned by any culture. 

How so?  Again, is this just an emotional attitude, or do you have some
intellectually convincing argument that shows a credible foundation for
what you prescribe?  

>Why should I be forced to have the children of 
>this country infested with beliefs that I hold so depraved?  Further, why 
>should I tolerate the use of tax dollars in an effort to support the 
>acceptability of homosexuality? 

Really the question you are asking is simply "why should I tolerate the 
use of tax dollars?" and not much more, because unless we are simply to
adopt only your personal sentiments as the criteria for making all laws
in our society, then we can have nothing that is indeed tax supported, 
since after accounting for each individuals disapproval of one thing or
another we find that everything is somehow objectionable.  I am not
dismissing the belief of many that there should be no taxation, no 
government, etc., rather I am just pointing out that this is a separate
issue to which homosexuality has no relevance.

>In regard to this particular issue, 
>August 4 1994 *would* be a better day if homosexuality could be eradicated. 

It is very unlikely that "homosexuality could be eradicated", but it is
indeed quite possible to cause enormous suffering for people who are 
homosexual, and also to cripple the reasoning ability of our children
as well as to deprive them of ideas and inforamtion in an attempt to
"eradicate" homosexuality.  How these consequences of the implementation
of an uncritically accepted set of beliefs can be construed to create for
us a better world, when such unproven beliefs themselves are not even shown
to do any demonstratable good, is not at all clear to me.

Again, on the issue of academic freedom, it may be a concept to which you
are, on the whole, hostile.  I suppose if that is so, you might want to 
make a larger case against academic freedom.  Of course, since this is a
group built around that concept, you will want to argue much more
convincingly than you have so far, since the readers of a.c.a-f are 
likely to have already thought about why they agree with this concept.

If academic freedom is indeed something which you also feel is necessary,
then you might want to provide arguments to support these attitudes of
yours within the context of academic freedom.  I hope you find these 
considerations useful to you in any further thought you might give to
this subject, but even if there is nothing in this post you find 
particularly valuable, I hope you at least continue to give some thought
to the subject.

Steve Nichols
What good do you imagine you can do for people by suppressing discourse?

